<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ClaFi - build settings MSBuild picks up on its own.

  MSBuild walks UP from each .vcxproj directory looking for Directory.Build.props,
  imports the first one it finds, and stops. The import happens after
  Microsoft.Common.props and before Microsoft.Cpp.props - that is, before the
  project body declares its ClCompile items - which is what makes the
  ItemDefinitionGroup below apply to every source without anyone editing a .vcxproj.

  PLACEMENT MATTERS: this only takes effect if the file sits in an ancestor
  directory of the .vcxproj that compiles these sources. See the notes at the end
  if yours lives somewhere else.
-->
<Project>

  <!--
    If a Directory.Build.props already exists further up, MSBuild would stop at this
    one and silently ignore it. Chain to it rather than shadowing it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ParentDirectoryBuildProps Condition="'$(ParentDirectoryBuildProps)' == ''">$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..'))</ParentDirectoryBuildProps>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(ParentDirectoryBuildProps)" Condition="'$(ParentDirectoryBuildProps)' != ''" />

  <!--
    Mirror the source tree inside the intermediate directory.

    By default MSBuild writes every object into one flat $(IntDir), so two sources
    sharing a basename collide - MSB8027. Controls\Grid.cppm and
    Controls\Grid\Engine\Grid.cppm both want Grid.obj.

    This is not a C++20 modules problem. MSVC derives BMI names from the *module*
    name (ClaFi.Controls.Grid.Engine-Grid.ifc), so .ifc files never collide. The
    collision is purely the object file, it hits plain .cpp identically, and it has
    behaved this way for twenty years. CMake and Bazel derive object paths from the
    source path already and need none of this.

    The trailing separator is required: it tells cl.exe that /Fo names a directory
    rather than a file. MSBuild's MakeDirsForCl target creates the subdirectories.
  -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <ObjectFileName>$(IntDir)%(RelativeDir)</ObjectFileName>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>

  <!--
    Opt-in build timing. Off unless the build is started with -p:ClaFiBuildTiming=true,
    so a normal build is unaffected.

    /Bt+ makes cl.exe print the wall time each phase spent on every translation unit -
    c1xx for the front end, c2 for the back end. /d1reportTime adds the front end's
    breakdown per template and per function, which is what identifies a single
    pathological instantiation rather than a broadly slow file.

    Both write to stdout, so the numbers land in whatever log the build is capturing.
  -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(ClaFiBuildTiming)' == 'true'">
    <ClCompile>
      <AdditionalOptions>%(AdditionalOptions) /Bt+ /d1reportTime</AdditionalOptions>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>

</Project>

<!--
  NOTES

  1. Sources outside the project directory.
     %(RelativeDir) for a file referenced as ..\..\Shared\Foo.cpp expands to a path
     containing "..\", so its object lands outside $(IntDir). Harmless when every
     ClCompile item sits at or below the .vcxproj directory, which is the normal
     arrangement. If you do pull sources in from above the project, give those
     specific items an explicit <ObjectFileName> in the .vcxproj.

  2. The .vcxproj is not below this file.
     If the project file sits above the framework folder, MSBuild never sees this
     file. Two options, both fine:
       - move Directory.Build.props up to the solution root, covering every project;
       - or rename it ClaFi.props and import it explicitly from the .vcxproj:
             <Import Project="..\ClaFi\ClaFi.props" />
         after the Microsoft.Cpp.props import. View > Property Manager writes that
         same import for you if you'd rather click.

  3. CMake builds.
     CMake generates .vcxproj files into the build directory, so this file is not an
     ancestor of them and will not be imported. That is fine - CMake gives each
     object a path-derived name and has no collision to fix.

  4. Verifying it took effect.
     Build, then look in the intermediate directory: you should see IntDir\Controls\
     and IntDir\Controls\Grid\Engine\ each holding their own Grid.obj. If everything
     is still flat, the file is not an ancestor of the .vcxproj - see note 2.
-->
